WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.275
Still images preserve discrete moments with potentially high detail.

00:00:04.275 --> 00:00:10.586
Video captures movement and sequence but creates more data and may reduce per-frame clarity.

00:00:10.586 --> 00:00:16.489
Choose the format that answers the objective rather than recording both by default.

00:00:16.489 --> 00:00:19.136
Altitude and angle shape perspective.

00:00:19.136 --> 00:00:23.818
Higher views cover more area but usually show less detail.

00:00:23.818 --> 00:00:30.129
Oblique views reveal building sides and terrain shape, while near-vertical views support plan-style comparison.

00:00:30.129 --> 00:00:33.996
Angle also changes scale, occlusion, and apparent distance.

00:00:33.996 --> 00:00:39.493
Field of view is the area visible through the camera or sensor.

00:00:39.493 --> 00:00:43.361
Lens choice, altitude, orientation, and cropping affect coverage.

00:00:43.361 --> 00:00:51.300
Record enough context to locate the subject without collecting a wide area that the mission does not need.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.796
Overlap helps mapping and before-and-after comparison by ensuring adjacent views share features.

00:00:56.796 --> 00:01:01.071
Consistent orientation, route, altitude, and capture interval improve repeatability.

00:01:01.071 --> 00:01:05.346
However, repeated collection should remain within the authorized purpose.

00:01:05.346 --> 00:01:07.178
Stable capture matters.

00:01:07.178 --> 00:01:13.896
Sudden motion, vibration, poor focus, haze, glare, and aggressive camera movement can make footage unusable.

00:01:13.896 --> 00:01:20.614
Pause or repeat a safe capture rather than rushing through a checklist while conditions deteriorate.

00:01:20.614 --> 00:01:26.518
Review a sample for exposure, focus, framing, storage, and metadata when operationally safe.

00:01:26.518 --> 00:01:33.643
Clear imagery does not remove uncertainty, but poor imagery can create false detail and unreliable interpretation.

00:01:33.643 --> 00:01:36.289
Quality begins with deliberate collection.

00:01:36.289 --> 00:01:44.228
Collection teams should prefer a small set of well-documented, usable images over a large volume of rushed footage.

00:01:44.228 --> 00:01:50.946
Excess data increases review effort and can obscure the evidence that actually answers the objective.
